    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030202 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030202">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0006790"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_1903510"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The chemical reactions and pathways involving heparin, any member of a group of glycosaminoglycans found mainly as an intracellular component of mast cells. They are similar to heparan sulfates but are of somewhat higher average Mr (6000-20000) and contain fewer N-acetyl groups and more N-sulfate and O-sulfate groups; they may be attached in the same manner to protein, forming proteoglycans. They consist predominantly of alternating alpha-(1-&gt;4)-linked D-galactose and N-acetyl-D-glucosamine-6-sulfate residues.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>heparin metabolism</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>heparan sulfate metabolic process</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heparin metabolic process</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
